  "text": "HOME/ DATA STRUCTURES/CHAPTER 01 CHAPTER 01 • DATA STRUCTURES INTRODUCTION TO DATA STRUCTURES QUANTUM UNIVERSITY B.TECH CSE SEMESTER 01 FULL NOTES IMPORTANT QUESTIONS 1.1 What is a Data Structure? Before we write a single line of code, we need to answer one question: how do we organize data so that our program can use it efficiently? That question is the entire subject in one sentence. DEFINITION A data structure is a systematic way of organizing, storing and managing data in a computer so that it can be accessed and modified efficiently — by both the program and the programmer. Think of it like this: the same books arranged in different ways give you completely different experiences. Sorted alphabetically? Finding a title is instant. Piled randomly? Good luck. The data is identical — the structure is what changes the cost of every operation. EXAMPLE Problem You have the marks of 60 students. You need (a) the average, (b) the topper, and (c) \"did anyone score exactly 77?\" Solution All three questions need the same data, but different structures make each question cheap or expensive: a simple array handles the average in one pass; finding the topper is another pass; and \"who scored 77\" is a search — fast if the array is sorted, slow if it isn't. Choosing the structure is choosing which operations you're optimizing for. 1.2 Why Data Structures Matter A classic formula you'll see in every textbook: FORMULA Program = Data Structure + Algorithm data (organized) + steps (to process it) = a working program The algorithm is the recipe; the data structure is how the ingredients are arranged on the counter. A great recipe with a chaotic kitchen still produces slow food. IMPORTANT For the exact same task, a better data structure can turn millions of operations into a handful. Searching 1 billion sorted records takes ~30 steps with binary search, but ~1,000,000,000 steps with a linear scan. Same data. Same problem. Wildly different cost. 1.3 Classification of Data Structures Data structures are classified along two main axes: Primitive vs Non-primitive — built-in language types vs structures we build out of them. Linear vs Non-linear — elements in a sequence vs elements in a hierarchy/network. Fig 1.1 — Classification of data structures One more distinction worth knowing for theory questions: Static — size fixed at compile time (array). Dynamic — size grows/shrinks at r"
