  "text": "HOME/ DATA STRUCTURES/CHAPTER 02 CHAPTER 02 • DATA STRUCTURES ARRAYS QUANTUM UNIVERSITY B.TECH CSE SEMESTER 01 FULL NOTES FORMULA SHEET 2.1 What is an Array? DEFINITION An array is a collection of elements of the same data type, stored in contiguous (side-by-side) memory locations, accessed by a single name and an index. Contiguity is the array's superpower and its curse. Because elements sit next to each other, the computer can jump to any element instantly — but it also means the whole block must be reserved in advance, and making room in the middle means physically shifting data. C COPY int marks[6] = {88, 92, 75, 63, 97, 81}; /* marks[0] is 88 ... marks[5] is 81 */ printf(\"%d\", marks[2]); /* prints 75 — O(1) access */ Fig 2.1 — One block of memory, index 0 to n−1 REMEMBER In C, array indices start at 0 and end at n−1. Writing marks[6] above is an out-of-bounds access — C won't stop you, it'll just read whatever garbage lives there. Classic bug, classic viva question. 2.2 Address Calculation — 1D Array Because memory is contiguous, the address of any element can be computed with arithmetic — no searching required. This is the most asked formula of the chapter: FORMULA Addr(A[i]) = Base + (i − LB) × w Base = address of first element · LB = lower bound (0 in C) · w = size of one element in bytes EXAMPLE Problem An int array A[10] starts at address 2000 (LB = 0, each int = 4 bytes). Find the address of A[6]. Solution Addr(A[6]) = 2000 + (6 − 0) × 4 = 2000 + 24 = 2024. Note how it doesn't matter whether the array has 10 or 10 million elements — the jump is always one multiplication and one addition. That's O(1) access. 2.3 Two-Dimensional Arrays A 2D array (a matrix) is stored in linear memory too — the compiler just flattens it row by row (row-major, used by C) or column by column (column-major, used by Fortran/MATLAB). FORMULA — ROW MAJOR Addr(A[i][j]) = Base + w × [(i − LR) × N + (j − LC)] N = number of columns · LR, LC = lower bounds (usually 0) FORMULA — COLUMN MAJOR Addr(A[i][j]) = Base + w × [(j − LC) × M + (i − LR)] M = number of rows EXAMPLE Problem A[4][5] of ints (4 bytes), base = 1000, 0-based. Find Addr(A[2][3]) in both layouts. Solution Row-major: 1000 + 4 × (2×5 + 3) = 1000 + 4×13 = 1052 Column-major: 1000 + 4 × (3×4 + 2) = 1000 + 4×14 = 1056 Same element, different address — the layout changes the maths, not the data. REMEMBER The #1 exam mist"
