  "text": "HOME/ DATA STRUCTURES/CHAPTER 03 CHAPTER 03 • DATA STRUCTURES LINKED LISTS QUANTUM UNIVERSITY B.TECH CSE SEMESTER 01 FULL NOTES IMPORTANT QUESTIONS 3.1 What is a Linked List? Arrays are a row of lockers: one block, fixed size, and rearranging anything means dragging lockers around. A linked list is a treasure hunt: each item knows where the next item lives, and the items can be scattered anywhere in memory. DEFINITION A linked list is a dynamic linear data structure in which elements (nodes) are stored at arbitrary memory locations and connected by pointers. Each node contains two parts: data + a pointer to the next node. Fig 3.1 — A singly linked list. Nodes may live anywhere; pointers give the order. REMEMBER If you lose the head pointer, you lose the entire list. There is no index, no formula, no way back — the only entry point is head. Guard it with your life. 3.2 The Node Structure In C, a node is a struct that contains the data and a pointer to another node of the same type — a self-referential structure: C COPY struct Node { int data; /* the payload */ struct Node *next; /* address of next node */ }; /* create a node on the heap at runtime */ struct Node *newNode(int value) { struct Node *p = (struct Node *) malloc(sizeof(struct Node)); p->data = value; p->next = NULL; return p; } Because nodes come from malloc (the heap) one at a time, the list can grow and shrink while the program runs — that's the \"dynamic\" in dynamic data structure. 3.3 Types of Linked Lists Table 3.1 — The four flavours TypeEach node hasLast node points toUse case Singly linkeddata + nextNULLSimple stacks/queues Doubly linkeddata + next + prevNULL (prev of head also NULL)Browser history, undo, text editors Circulardata + nextBack to HEADRound-robin scheduling, music playlists Doubly circulardata + next + prevBoth directions loopMedia players, schedulers IMPORTANT In a doubly linked list you can walk backwards for free (O(1) prev), but every node pays one extra pointer's memory and every insert/delete must maintain two links instead of one. Nothing is free in data structures. 3.4 Operations Traversal — O(n) Start at head, follow next until you hit NULL. There is literally no other way to reach node #k: C COPY struct Node *p = head; while (p != NULL) { printf(\"%d -> \", p->data); p = p->next; /* hop to the next node */ } printf(\"NULL\\n\"); Insertion at the beginning — O(1) The cheape"
