  "text": "HOME/ ENGINEERING MATHEMATICS – I/CHAPTER 01 CHAPTER 01 • ENGINEERING MATHEMATICS – I LIMITS & CONTINUITY QUANTUM UNIVERSITY B.TECH CSE SEMESTER 01 FULL NOTES IMPORTANT QUESTIONS 1.1 The Idea of a Limit Calculus begins with a deceptively simple question: what value is a function getting close to? Not \"what value does it reach\" — what it approaches. The limit is the destination, even if the function never actually arrives. DEFINITION Informal: limx→a f(x) = L means f(x) can be made arbitrarily close to L by taking x sufficiently close to a (from either side, but not necessarily at a). Formal (ε–δ): for every ε > 0 there exists δ > 0 such that 0 < |x − a| < δ implies |f(x) − L| < ε. EXAMPLE Problem Investigate limx→2 (x² − 4)/(x − 2). Solution Direct substitution gives 0/0 — indeterminate, undefined at x = 2. But watch the approach: x = 1.9 → f(x) = 3.9 x = 1.99 → f(x) = 3.99 x = 2.01 → f(x) = 4.01 x = 2.1 → f(x) = 4.1 Both sides squeeze toward 4. Factorise to confirm: (x²−4)/(x−2) = (x−2)(x+2)/(x−2) = x + 2 → 4 as x → 2. The hole at x = 2 doesn't matter — limits only care about the neighbourhood. 1.2 Left and Right Hand Limits Approaching a from the left (x → a⁻) and from the right (x → a⁺) can give different answers — and that single fact decides whether a function is continuous: FORMULA limx→a f(x) = L ⟺ LHL = RHL = L Left Hand Limit = lim(x→a⁻) f(x) · Right Hand Limit = lim(x→a⁺) f(x) IMPORTANT The limit exists only if LHL = RHL. If they differ, the limit does not exist (DNE) — no amount of clever algebra saves it. For piecewise functions, always compute both sides separately at the joining point. 1.3 Algebra of Limits Table 1.1 — If lim f = L and lim g = M, then… RuleStatement Sumlim [f + g] = L + M Differencelim [f − g] = L − M Productlim [f · g] = L · M Quotientlim [f / g] = L / M (M ≠ 0) Constant multiplelim [k·f] = k·L Compositionlim g(f(x)) = g(L) if g is continuous at L 1.4 Standard Limits (MEMORISE) FORMULA SHEET limx→0 sin x / x = 1 • limx→0 tan x / x = 1 limx→0 (1 − cos x) / x = 0 • limx→0 (1 − cos x)/x² = ½ all trigonometric standard limits — x in radians, always FORMULA SHEET limx→0 (1 + x)1/x = e • limn→∞ (1 + 1/n)n = e limx→0 ln(1 + x)/x = 1 • limx→0 (ex − 1)/x = 1 limx→0 (ax − 1)/x = ln a • limx→0 ((1+x)n − 1)/x = n the exponential & logarithmic family — these six settle most exam questions REMEMBER The sin x / x family only works when the d"
