  "text": "HOME/ ENGINEERING MATHEMATICS – I/CHAPTER 02 CHAPTER 02 • ENGINEERING MATHEMATICS – I DIFFERENTIATION & APPLICATIONS QUANTUM UNIVERSITY B.TECH CSE SEMESTER 01 FULL NOTES FORMULA SHEET 2.1 The Derivative as a Limit DEFINITION The derivative of f at x is the limit of the average rate of change as the interval shrinks to zero: FORMULA f′(x) = limh→0 [f(x + h) − f(x)] / h also written dy/dx — \"a tiny change in y per tiny change in x\" Geometric meaning: the derivative is the slope of the tangent line at a point on the curve. Average rate = slope of a chord; the derivative is what that chord becomes when its two endpoints collapse into one. Fig 2.1 — As R slides toward P along the curve, the chord QR turns into the tangent at P. EXAMPLE Problem Find f′(x) from first principles for f(x) = x². Solution f′(x) = limh→0 [(x+h)² − x²]/h = lim [2xh + h²]/h = lim (2x + h) = 2x. \"From first principles\" = use the limit definition directly. Exams ask for it precisely to check you know where derivatives come from. 2.2 Standard Derivatives (FORMULA SHEET) Table 2.1 — Differentiate anything, eventually f(x)f′(x)f(x)f′(x) xⁿn·xⁿ⁻¹sin xcos x cos x−sin xtan xsec²x cot x−cosec²xsec xsec x · tan x cosec x−cosec x · cot xeˣeˣ aˣaˣ ln aln x1/x sin⁻¹x1/√(1−x²)tan⁻¹x1/(1+x²) constant0uvu′v + uv′ REMEMBER Every \"co-\" function's derivative carries a minus sign: (cos)′ = −sin, (cot)′ = −cosec², (cosec)′ = −cosec·cot. If your derivative of a co-function came out positive, pause and re-check. 2.3 The Rules PRODUCT RULE (u · v)′ = u′v + u v′ \"first d-second + second d-first\" QUOTIENT RULE (u / v)′ = (u′v − u v′) / v² \"low d-high minus high d-low, over low squared\" · v ≠ 0 CHAIN RULE dy/dx = (dy/du) · (du/dx) derivative of outside(at inside) × derivative of inside EXAMPLE Problem Differentiate (a) x² sin x (b) x/(x+1) (c) sin(x²). Solution (a) Product rule: 2x·sin x + x²·cos x (b) Quotient rule: [1·(x+1) − x·1]/(x+1)² = 1/(x+1)² (c) Chain rule: cos(x²) · 2x = 2x cos(x²) 2.4 Implicit & Parametric Differentiation When y is tangled inside an equation (implicit) or both x and y depend on a parameter t (parametric), you don't solve for y — you differentiate the relationship: IMPLICIT F(x, y) = 0 ⟹ dy/dx = −Fx / Fy differentiate both sides w.r.t. x, treating y as a function of x (chain rule on every y-term) PARAMETRIC dy/dx = (dy/dt) / (dx/dt) x = f(t), y = g(t) — divide the two rates EXAMPLE x² + y"
