  "text": "HOME/ ENGINEERING MATHEMATICS – I/CHAPTER 03 CHAPTER 03 • ENGINEERING MATHEMATICS – I INTEGRATION QUANTUM UNIVERSITY B.TECH CSE SEMESTER 01 FULL NOTES FORMULA SHEET 3.1 What is Integration? Differentiation asks \"what's the slope?\" Integration asks the reverse question: \"I know the rate — what was the function?\" It's differentiation played backwards, plus one beautiful geometric meaning: area under the curve. DEFINITION Integration is the process of finding a function whose derivative is the given function. The result is called an antiderivative or indefinite integral: FORMULA ∫ f(x) dx = F(x) + C where F′(x) = f(x) indefinite = family of curves (shifted vertically) · definite = a number REMEMBER Never drop the +C in an indefinite integral. Because the derivative of any constant is 0, infinitely many antiderivatives exist — the + C is the difference between \"all of them\" and \"a lucky guess\". Exams dock a mark every single time. 3.2 Standard Integrals (FORMULA SHEET) Table 3.1 — The bread and butter f(x)∫ f(x) dxf(x)∫ f(x) dx xⁿ (n ≠ −1)xⁿ⁺¹/(n+1) + C1/xln|x| + C eˣeˣ + Caˣaˣ/ln a + C sin x−cos x + Ccos xsin x + C sec²xtan x + Ccosec²x−cot x + C sec x · tan xsec x + C1/(1+x²)tan⁻¹x + C 1/√(1−x²)sin⁻¹x + Ctan xln|sec x| + C cot xln|sin x| + C1/(x²+a²)(1/a)tan⁻¹(x/a) + C 1/√(a²−x²)sin⁻¹(x/a) + C1/(x²−a²)(1/2a)ln|(x−a)/(x+a)| + C REMEMBER Integration reverses the sign of the \"co-\" derivatives: ∫sin = −cos but ∫cos = sin. If you memorised the derivative table (Ch 2, Table 2.1), you already know this table — just read it right-to-left. 3.3 Method 1 — Substitution Spot a function and its derivative living in the same integral. Substitute the inner function as u, and the mess collapses: FORMULA ∫ f(g(x)) · g′(x) dx = ∫ f(u) du (u = g(x)) chain rule, run in reverse EXAMPLE Problem ∫ 2x · cos(x²) dx Solution Notice 2x is the derivative of x². Let u = x², du = 2x dx: ∫ cos(u) du = sin(u) + C = sin(x²) + C Trigger to look for: whose derivative is sitting in the integrand? That's your u. 3.4 Method 2 — Integration by Parts (ILATE) For products of unrelated families (a polynomial × a trig function, an exponential × a log…): FORMULA ∫ u · v dx = u ∫v dx − ∫ [u′ · (∫v dx)] dx shorthand: ∫u v = u∫v − ∫u′∫v — \"integrate by parts\" Choosing which factor is u (the one you differentiate) matters enormously. The ILATE priority order decides: I Inverse trig (sin⁻¹, tan⁻¹…) — highest"
